A||N||27||fastonpumpgas||custom subwoofer||||1||
Z||000000||fastonpumpgas||09-19-1999||04:52 PM||||Any Ideas on how or were i put a 10 or 12 inch subwoofer in my car....?????||||reg||1
Z||000001||87FieroGT||09-19-1999||05:17 PM||||I don't know about a 10"or 12" in the car, but have a custom setup with an 8" under the dash in the location where the stock subwoofer went. It sounds pretty good write now even with the factory stereo, and I know will sound better when I installed a after market one. If you would like to know how it was done E-Mail me.||||reg||6
Z||000002||yellowFormula||09-19-1999||08:47 PM||||I think there are only 2 places to put a 10" or 12" sub in the cabin, and those would be behind either the passenger seat or in the passenger footwell. There is a brand of subs I can't remember the name but they make super slim subs at competition level, (so I've heard anyway) but they are real expensive(like 260 for a 10"). I have a 12" solo-baric behind the passenger seat, which needs to be moved all the way up unless you are willing to cut the carpet behind the seats. I found this better because atleast it leaves SOME room for the passengers feet. <P><BR>-jeff||||reg||12
Z||000003||spike||09-20-1999||08:30 AM||||A few years ago I had installed a 250 Watt RMS<BR>Amp and a pair (yes 2) of Kenwood 10" Subs all connected to the stock radio. I removed the rear speakers and just kept the front ones for my mids & highs. My set-up did not cause any less seat travel. I know this is the best way to install subs in a Fiero. Looks really custom. I would like to build this system and sell them. I know many fiero owners would buy this system. Next summer when I get my new Fiero GT I will attend various shows and display my system. Trust me it's worth waiting for if you like big BASS.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula||09-20-1999||12:40 PM||||I have a 10" sub where the spare tire goes.  The spare is in the trunk behind the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||biggdaddy||09-20-1999||11:48 PM||||If you do not mind losing the little bit of space in the middle console between the seats, just install it on there. That could probably hold a 10" no problem...maybe even a 12"?  E-mail me if you want a picture of what this looks like.||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||09-21-1999||03:22 PM||||the fiero certainly does represent a challange for installing sub's.  My buddy and I use to be in the stereo business, also both owning 86.5 GT's.  Needless to say, we learned a thing or two about speaker placement in Fiero's.  If you absolutely must have the largest subwoofer available, then your only real options are the footwell or behind the seat.  However, we were able to obtain exellent base from 6.5" band pass tubes positioned directly in front of the seat, on the floor board (this made the seat feel slightly longer).  Combine this with mid  range and tweeters in the factory locations.  Have you eaver felt your heart pulse to a song?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fastonpumpgas||09-21-1999||05:13 PM||||anybody have pictures of there installations?||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||09-21-1999||10:27 PM||||I have an 8 inch sub in a 12"x12"x5" box behind the passenger seat in my Batmobile. The 85 GT I just recently bought already had a nice stereo installed. It has an 8 inch sub in the glove box area. The compartment door was removed and a box built to fit into the space. I took pics of with and without the custom made grills that match the interior color.<P> <A HREF="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/special" TARGET=_blank>http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/special</A> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 09-22-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||spike||09-22-1999||09:08 AM||||The way I had my 2 10" is diffinitly the way to go for BIG BASS. My box costed about 75.00<BR>to build plus the time of course. It also looked like it came with the car due to the fact that I had covered it with the matching gray vynle. The box fit so tight up against the inside molding of the car. Perfect fit and look. Maybe I will try to stuff 2 12" this time around when I get my Fiero GT, they should fit fine with a little box modification.||||reg||1
Z||000010||spike||09-22-1999||09:08 AM||||The way I had my 2 10" is diffinitly the way to go for BIG BASS. My box costed about 75.00<BR>to build plus the time of course. It also looked like it came with the car due to the fact that I had covered it with the matching gray vynle. The box fit so tight up against the inside molding of the car. Perfect fit and look. Maybe I will try to stuff 2 12" this time around when I get my Fiero GT, they should fit fine with a little box modification.||||reg||1
Z||000011||bHooper||09-22-1999||02:54 PM||||Spike,  do not forget that subs need to have a specific amount of air volume behind them, in order to operate at maximum efficency.  many people make the mistake of stuffing a larger woofer in a small box, suffocating the speaker.  the best way around this, in tight area's, is to utilize a bandpass box.  this is a box that is ported on one side to allow for more air and surface volume.  <P>there may also be a long term problem with mounting a sub in the glove box.  the magnet could couse the computer to malfunction, just a thought.||||reg||12
Z||000012||Formula||09-22-1999||08:16 PM||||I have a sony Xplod 10" sub where the spare tire goes in a hatch style box.  I sounds just as good as a JL audio 12" sub in the trunk of a subaru. (Just for comparison).<BR>It is crystal clear sounding. I have a 400 watt Jensen amp behind the seat using all rockford fosgate wiring.  You do have to drill a new hole above the cpu and push the power wire straight down under the rear window. But it sounds great......And a plus is you don't need the windows down to bass through town!||||reg||1
Z||000013||sluppy123||10-01-1999||12:03 PM||||here's a pic of my sub install, I believe it is the best way to go for a decent sounding system. <P> <IMG SRC="Http://members.aol.com/fierogt321/myhomepage/sub90.bmp"> <P>BTW. It's a 8" sub||||reg||1
Z||000014||Lynx||10-01-1999||12:27 PM||||If you really want to to get the best sound and not lose any room inside the car, you have to put subwoofers in the front trunk. But to do it the right way it is very costly. You have to have a stereo store build a special costom bandpass inclosure in the front and put port tubes underneth the car in to the cabin. This is how a few fiero's in IASCA do it. It sounds really good too becasause the bass is coming from the front of the car and not into your back.||||reg||12
Z||000015||spike||10-01-1999||02:52 PM||||My box is a little small on the volume size that the 2 10" subs needed. I had made 2 small 1" ports to aid the performance of these subs.<BR>I found that they even sounded better when the ports were blocked. They were not able to go as low as the advertised figure, but trust me there was still plenty bass to shake the car pritty good. I was playing with my system outside my brothers house and he came out yelling at me to turn it down. The bass was shaking the heck out of his front window without any problems. I still say this is the best Sub System for the Fiero hands down. All for about 600.00 cdn. 1 sub is prob. enough but 2 subs are much better. The best thing is the installation was clean and everything matched the inside of the car. It did look perfect and right at home. Everyone I meet was really impressed with my system considering I did it all myself. It looked like a custom made installation.||||reg||1
Z||000016||sluppy123||10-01-1999||03:32 PM||||My sub system only ran me about $100 U.S. and the best thing is I can still here that wonderful sound coming from the back through the $400 exhaust system, and the $2000 of new engine. grrrrrrrrrrr. <P>that's my 2-cents, Brian||||reg||1
Z||000017||grizco||02-20-2000||03:17 AM||||sluppy123 what are the dimensions of your sub box. I'm building one for my fiero and was considering placing it in the passenger side footwell.||||reg||5
